# ---------------- Introduction ----------------
H('1. Introduction')
RICH([('Colistin returned to clinical use because there was little else left. Decades after it had been abandoned on grounds of nephrotoxicity, the spread of carbapenem-resistant ',False),('Enterobacterales',True),(' forced clinicians back onto the polymyxins for infections that no longer responded to anything else (Poirel, Jayol, and Nordmann, 2017). Resistance was for a long time thought to arise only through chromosomal mutation, and therefore to stay within the lineage in which it appeared. That assumption ended with the description of ',False),('mcr-1',True),(' on a conjugative plasmid in ',False),('Escherichia coli',True),(' from Chinese pigs (Liu et al., 2016). Eleven further families have since been named, from mcr-2 to mcr-12 (Xavier et al., 2016; Carattoli et al., 2017; Yin et al., 2017; X. Wang et al., 2018; Yang et al., 2018; Carroll et al., 2019; C. Wang et al., 2020), and they have been found in food animals, in retail meat, in environmental water and in patients on four continents.',False)])
RICH([('All ',False),('mcr',True),(' products belong to the alkaline phosphatase superfamily and act as phosphoethanolamine transferases. They attach a phosphoethanolamine group to the 1 and 4′ phosphates of lipid A, which lowers the net negative charge of the outer membrane and weakens the electrostatic attraction that the polymyxins depend on. The same chemistry is performed by chromosomal enzymes that long predate the clinical use of colistin. EptA modifies lipid A, EptB modifies the outer 3-deoxy-D-',False),('manno',True),('-octulosonic acid residue of the inner core (Reynolds et al., 2005), CptA modifies the heptose of the core, and EptC of ',False),('Campylobacter jejuni',True),(' modifies both lipid A and the flagellar rod protein FlgG (Cullen et al., 2012). In ',False),('Neisseria meningitidis',True),(' the homologue LptA is required for resistance to polymyxin (Tzeng et al., 2005; Wanty et al., 2013).',False)])
RICH([('Structural work has shown how close the relationship is. The periplasmic domain of MCR-1 adopts the alkaline phosphatase fold, binds zinc, and uses a threonine nucleophile that becomes transiently phosphorylated during catalysis (Hu et al., 2016; Ma et al., 2016). The catalytic domain of MCR-2 has the same arrangement (Coates et al., 2017), and so does the EptC homologue of E. coli, which binds zinc in the same way but lacks the disulfide bonds and does not confer resistance (Zhao et al., 2019). The fold, the metal site and the nucleophile are also present in the neisserial LptA (Wanty et al., 2013). Nothing in the enzyme distinguishes a mobilised transferase from a chromosomal one. What distinguishes them is the DNA around the gene.',False)])
RICH([('Individual donors have been proposed for several families. ',False),('Moraxella',True),(' species carry chromosomal genes close enough to ',False),('mcr-1',True),(' and ',False),('mcr-2',True),(' to be regarded as their probable source (AbuOun et al., 2017; Kieffer et al., 2017), and ',False),('mcr-4',True),(' was noted on discovery to resemble a chromosomal protein of ',False),('Shewanella frigidimarina',True),(' (Carattoli et al., 2017). Those observations were made one family at a time, against whichever sequences were available when each family was described. They have not been brought together in a single analysis, and three questions have therefore stayed open. Do the families share one origin or many? Was the catalytic machinery complete before mobilisation or assembled after it? And which chromosomal genes are close enough to the mobilised ones to be plausible sources of the next event?',False)])
RICH([('This study addresses those questions. Chromosomal phosphoethanolamine transferases were retrieved across the breadth of the ',False),('Enterobacterales',True),(' and from the taxa implicated as donors, analysed together with the reference alleles of the eleven ',False),('mcr',True),(' families that have one and with an outgroup from the wider superfamily, and examined for phylogenetic placement, for the conservation of the catalytic apparatus, for ancestral state at the nodes where mobilisation must have occurred, for the strength of selection, for genomic context, and for whether reference-based surveillance would detect them.',False)])

