RICH([('Nucleotide sequences for the reference allele of each ',False),('mcr',True),(' family were taken from the NCBI Bacterial Antimicrobial Resistance Reference Gene Catalog (Feldgarden et al., 2021) as distributed with ABRicate v1.0.1 (https://github.com/tseemann/abricate, database snapshot of 2024-01-24). Every family holding a reference allele in that release was included. This gave ',False),('mcr-1.1',True),(' (NG_050417.1), ',False),('mcr-2.1',True),(' (NG_051171.1), ',False),('mcr-3.1',True),(' (NG_055505.1), ',False),('mcr-4.1',True),(' (NG_057470.1), ',False),('mcr-5.1',True),(' (NG_055658.1), ',False),('mcr-6.1',True),(' (NG_055781.1), ',False),('mcr-7.1',True),(' (NG_056413.1), ',False),('mcr-8.1',True),(' (NG_061399.1), ',False),('mcr-10.1',True),(' (NG_066767.1) and ',False),('mcr-12.1',True),(' (NG_245195.1). The catalogue holds no entry for ',False),('mcr-11',True),(', which could therefore not be included, and no entry for ',False),('mcr-9',True),(' under that designation, so the reference allele of ',False),('mcr-9',True),(' was taken from the original description (MK070339; Carroll et al., 2019). All eleven sequences translated without internal stop codons. For the analyses of selection within families, every allele of each family held in the same catalogue release was retrieved, giving 37 alleles of ',False),('mcr-1',True),(', 41 of ',False),('mcr-3',True),(', nine of ',False),('mcr-4',True),(', eight of ',False),('mcr-2',True),(', six each of ',False),('mcr-8',True),(' and ',False),('mcr-10',True),(', and five of ',False),('mcr-5',True),('.',False)])

H('2.2. Chromosomal and related transferases',3)
RICH([('Chromosomal homologues were retrieved from the NCBI RefSeq non-redundant protein collection (O’Leary et al., 2016) using 45 taxon queries, 35 of them genus-level taxa of ',False),('Enterobacterales',True),(' and ten of taxa outside the order that have been implicated as donors or that carry well-characterised paralogues, namely ',False),('Moraxella',True),(', ',False),('Faucicola',True),(' (into which ',False),('Moraxella osloensis',True),(' has been transferred on taxogenomic grounds; Sbissi et al., 2025; T\u00f8njum et al., 2026), ',False),('Acinetobacter',True),(', ',False),('Shewanella',True),(', ',False),('Aeromonas',True),(', ',False),('Vibrio',True),(', ',False),('Neisseria',True),(', ',False),('Campylobacter',True),(', ',False),('Pseudomonas',True),(' and ',False),('Burkholderia',True),('. Records were retained if the annotation identified a phosphoethanolamine transferase, a lipid A phosphoethanolamine transferase or a sulfatase-family protein of the appropriate length. Within each query the retained records were clustered at approximately 80% identity and one representative was kept, which gave 150 chromosomal proteins. The 45 queries and the 150 retained proteins are not in one-to-one correspondence. Retention was decided on annotation rather than on the query taxon, so records of a few genera outside the query list were returned and kept, among them Cupriavidus. The retained set represents 44 genera, and 22 of the records carry annotation only above genus level.',False)])
RICH([('Clustering was used for retrieval only. Because it can remove the closest relative of a mobilised allele, the identity analyses were repeated against an unclustered set. Every reviewed and unreviewed UniProtKB (UniProt Consortium, 2025) entry annotated as a phosphoethanolamine transferase, an EptA-like protein or an MCR-like protein was retrieved for ',False),('Moraxella',True),(', ',False),('Faucicola',True),(', ',False),('Shewanella',True),(', ',False),('Aeromonas',True),(', ',False),('Kluyvera',True),(' and ',False),('Buttiauxella',True),(', giving 28 further sequences. Five members of the wider alkaline phosphatase superfamily of ',False),('E. coli',True),(' were added to root the tree: OpgB (P39401), AslA (P25549), YidJ (P31447) and YdeN (P77318) as outgroup, and OpgE (P75785) as a reference for the paralogue group described below. After removal of three sequences that were exact duplicates of others already present, the working dataset held 191 proteins. The 161-sequence subset made up of the RefSeq proteins and the reference alleles was used for the primary phylogenetic analysis, and the full 191-sequence set for the identity analyses and for a rooted reanalysis.',False)])
RICH([('One sequence, WP_261396260.1 of ',False),('Citrobacter freundii',True),(', is 400 residues against a median of 546 across the chromosomal and related proteins and is the only record below 450. It was retained in the alignment and the tree, where its placement is informative, and excluded from every calculation of identity to a mobilised allele and from the ranking of candidate donors.',False)])

H('2.3. Alignment and phylogenetic inference',3)
RICH([('Proteins were aligned with FAMSA (Deorowicz et al., 2016) as implemented in pyfamsa v0.7.0. The 161-sequence alignment held 808 columns and the 191-sequence alignment 1038. Poorly aligned positions were removed with trimAl (Capella-Gutiérrez et al., 2009) in pytrimal v0.8.5 under the automated1 heuristic, leaving 313 and 444 columns respectively. Maximum-likelihood trees were inferred with IQ-TREE v2.3.6 (Minh et al., 2020). Model selection used ModelFinder (Kalyaanamoorthy et al., 2017) across the LG, WAG, JTT, VT, Blosum62, Dayhoff, cpREV, rtREV and PMB matrices, each with and without empirical amino acid frequencies, with and without a proportion of invariant sites, and with discrete gamma and free-rate models of among-site rate variation. LG+F+I+R6, which combines the LG exchangeability matrix (Le and Gascuel, 2008) with empirical frequencies, a proportion of invariant sites and six free rate categories, was selected by the Bayesian information criterion and used for the primary analysis. Branch support came from 1000 ultrafast bootstrap replicates (Hoang et al., 2018) and 1000 replicates of the SH-like approximate likelihood ratio test (Guindon et al., 2010). The expanded alignment was analysed under LG+F+R4 with the four superfamily proteins constrained as the outgroup; the simplified rate model was used to keep the larger analysis tractable.',False)])
RICH([('Monophyly of the mobilised families was tested explicitly. A constraint tree placing the eleven mobilised proteins in one clade and leaving the remaining relationships unresolved was supplied to a second maximum-likelihood search under the same model. The search was stopped after 60 rounds of topology optimisation, at which point successive improvements had fallen below one log-likelihood unit. The constrained and unconstrained trees were then compared on the same alignment with the approximately unbiased test (Shimodaira, 2002), the Kishino-Hasegawa and Shimodaira-Hasegawa tests and their weighted variants, and the expected likelihood weight, using 10,000 RELL replicates.',False)])

H('2.4. Identity, domain architecture and active-site mapping',3)
RICH([('Pairwise amino acid identity was calculated over columns at which both sequences carried a residue in the untrimmed alignment of the 191-sequence set. Hydropathy was computed on the Kyte and Doolittle scale over a sliding window of 19 residues; contiguous runs with a mean above 1.6 were merged and counted as predicted membrane-spanning segments. Sixteen positions reported from the MCR-1 crystal structures (Hu et al., 2016; Ma et al., 2016) were mapped through the alignment with Biopython v1.88 (Cock et al., 2009) and their occupancy tabulated. Six of the sixteen carry the same residue in every sequence in the dataset and are therefore uninformative for any comparison between groups; the remaining ten vary and are treated separately throughout.',False)])

H('2.5. Ancestral sequence reconstruction',3)
RICH([('Marginal ancestral states were reconstructed with IQ-TREE on the untrimmed alignment under the selected model, with the topology and branch lengths of the maximum-likelihood tree held fixed. For each mobilised lineage the state was read at the stem node, that is at the node the lineage shares with its chromosomal relatives, rather than at the crown of the mobilised clade. Reconstruction was repeated on the outgroup-rooted topology of the 191-sequence analysis to check that the result did not depend on the position of the root. States are reported only for the ten positions that vary in the dataset, with their posterior probabilities.',False)])

H('2.6. Selection',3)
RICH([('Codon alignments were generated by threading coding sequences onto the corresponding protein alignments. Rates of synonymous and non-synonymous substitution were estimated by maximum likelihood with codeml from PAML v4.10.10 (Yang, 2007) under the F3x4 codon frequency model. Pairwise estimates between families were used first to establish whether synonymous sites retained usable information. They did not, so between-family estimates are not reported. Within families, where synonymous divergence is low, the one-ratio model M0 was fitted to each allele alignment on a neighbour-joining topology. For mcr-1, the family with the largest set of closely related alleles, site models were fitted and compared by likelihood ratio test, M1a against M2a and M7 against M8 (Yang et al., 2000), with posterior identification of sites by the Bayes empirical Bayes procedure. Results from the site models are interpreted with the limits on accuracy and power at low divergence in mind (Anisimova et al., 2002).',False)])

H('2.7. Genomic context and mobile elements',3)
RICH([('Genomic context was examined for the original deposits of ',False),('mcr-1',True),(' (KP347127), ',False),('mcr-2',True),(' (LT598652), ',False),('mcr-4',True),(' (MF543359), ',False),('mcr-5',True),(' (KY807921), ',False),('mcr-7.1',True),(' (MG267386) and ',False),('mcr-8',True),(' (MG736312), and for four chromosomal donor loci. Annotated flat files were taken from the European Nucleotide Archive and features were read within a window extending approximately 10 kb either side of the transferase gene. Predicted open reading frames from the chromosomal windows were searched against the transposase profile hidden Markov models distributed with ISEScan (Xie and Tang, 2017) using pyhmmer v0.12.3 (Eddy, 2011). The window is narrower than the 30 to 50 kb used in dedicated studies of composite transposons, and the negative results below are reported as properties of that window.',False)])

H('2.8. Detection by reference-based methods',3)
RICH([('To test whether current surveillance would recognise the chromosomal homologues, profile hidden Markov models were built from the catalogued allele sets of the ',False),('mcr',True),(' families. The NCBI and ResFinder catalogues together hold 219 alleles across eleven families. A profile was built for each of the eight families with at least three alleles, which are ',False),('mcr-1',True),(', ',False),('mcr-2',True),(', ',False),('mcr-3',True),(', ',False),('mcr-4',True),(', ',False),('mcr-5',True),(', ',False),('mcr-8',True),(', ',False),('mcr-9',True),(' and ',False),('mcr-10',True),('. The three remaining families hold one or two alleles each and cannot be modelled, which is itself part of the result. For each family the detection threshold was set at the lowest score achieved by a catalogued allele of that family, which is the most permissive threshold a reference-based tool could adopt without admitting members of other families. The 176 chromosomal and related proteins of the dataset were scored against all profiles, together with the two records that had been removed as exact duplicates of mobilised alleles, 178 in all.',False)])

