P('All sequences analysed are public records held by NCBI, the European Nucleotide Archive and UniProtKB. Accessions for the chromosomal and related proteins, with their ranking by identity to the closest mobilised family, are given in Supplementary Table S1, and accessions for the mobilised alleles are given in section 2.1. The analysis scripts are deposited at [repository URL] and archived with the persistent identifier [DOI]. The trimmed and untrimmed alignments, the maximum-likelihood tree with both support measures, the ultrafast bootstrap replicates, the constrained tree, the output of the topology tests, the reconstructed ancestral states and the codeml output files accompany this article as named additional files. Each figure legend ends with the name of the script that produced it.',indent=False)

# ---------------- Results ----------------
H('3. Results')

H('3.1. Three paralogue groups, with every mobilised family in one of them',3)
RICH([('The 161 proteins of the primary analysis resolved into three groups corresponding to the three ',False),('E. coli',True),(' paralogues (Figure 1). The group anchored by EptA held 76 sequences, 65 of them chromosomal and 11 mobilised. The group anchored by EptB held 36 and the group anchored by CptA held 49. No sequence fell outside the three. Support for the groups was uneven: the CptA group had 99.3 SH-aLRT and 100 ultrafast bootstrap, the EptB group 47.7 and 96, and the EptA group 73.7 and 61. The weakness of the last value reflects the difficulty of resolving the deepest split in a family this divergent, and it is the reason the claims below rest on tests rather than on support values alone.',False)])
RICH([('The group anchored by CptA is not homogeneous. When OpgE of ',False),('E. coli',True),(' (P75785) was added, 35 of the 49 sequences fell with it and only 14 with CptA and EptC. OpgE modifies osmoregulated periplasmic glucans rather than lipopolysaccharide, so 35 of the 150 chromosomal transferases, just under a quarter, act on a substrate that has nothing to do with the charge of the outer membrane. That distinction matters for interpreting the active-site tables below, and we keep the two subgroups separate from this point on.',False)])

