CON="A constrained maximum-likelihood tree forcing the eleven mobilised proteins into one clade reached a log-likelihood of {c:.2f} against {m:.2f} for the unconstrained tree, a difference of {d:.1f} units. The approximately unbiased test rejected the constrained topology at p = 8.1 x 10-6. The Kishino-Hasegawa and Shimodaira-Hasegawa tests and their weighted variants gave p < 0.001, and the expected likelihood weight of the constrained tree was 7.2 x 10-283. A single origin for the mobilised families can therefore be excluded, and the nine lineages recovered in the unconstrained tree represent at least nine separate transfers out of chromosomes and into mobile elements.".format(c=T['con_lnl'],m=T['ml_lnl'],d=T['deltaL'])
P(CON)

RICH([('The expanded analysis gave the same answer in a stronger form. With the 28 unclustered records added and the tree rooted on the superfamily outgroup, ten separate mobilised lineages were recovered rather than nine, because the chromosomal ',False),('Moraxella',True),(' protein A0A220T316 falls between MCR-1.1 and the MCR-2, MCR-6 pair. MCR-1.1 and that protein are sisters at 99.8 SH-aLRT and 100 ultrafast bootstrap, MCR-3.1 and A0ABX6NRT3 of ',False),('Aeromonas media',True),(' at 100 and 100, and MCR-2.1 and MCR-6.1 at 95.6 and 100. The four superfamily proteins used as outgroup did not themselves form a clade, so the root position is approximate and we treat the rooted analysis as a check on the primary one rather than as a replacement for it. In the same tree all 35 sequences annotated here as OpgE-like fall in a clade with OpgE at 93.9 SH-aLRT and 100 ultrafast bootstrap, a clade that also admits four CptA-like sequences; the tightest clade containing OpgE and no CptA-like sequence holds 29 of them at 99.9 and 100.',False)])
H('3.3. Each family has a distinct closest chromosomal relative',3)
RICH([('Identity to the nearest full-length chromosomal protein varied more than twofold across the families (Table 2), leaving aside for the moment the two records that are identical to MCR-5.1 and MCR-6.1. MCR-1.1 was 98.7% identical to A0A220T316 of ',False),('Moraxella',True),(' sp. MSG13C03, MCR-3.1 was 95.0% identical to A0ABX6NRT3 of ',False),('Aeromonas media',True),(', MCR-9.1 was 87.4% identical to WP_232921000.1 of ',False),('B. cochleicola',True),(' and MCR-10.1 84.6% to the same protein. MCR-6.1 reached 82.3% and MCR-2.1 81.0% against the ',False),('Moraxella',True),(' record, MCR-4.1 81.5% against A3D4D9 of ',False),('Shewanella baltica',True),(' OS155, and MCR-7.1 78.3% against WP_499669208.1 of ',False),('Aeromonas jandaei',True),('. Three families were much more isolated: MCR-5.1 at 45.1%, MCR-8.1 at 43.8% and MCR-12.1 at 40.6%.',False)])
RICH([('Two chromosomal records were identical to catalogued alleles over their entire length. A0A220T317 of ',False),('Moraxella',True),(' sp. MSG47C17 matches MCR-6.1 residue for residue, and A0A2R3NW71 of ',False),('Aeromonas hydrophila',True),(' matches MCR-5.1. Neither record is annotated as a resistance determinant. A chromosomal gene identical to a mobilised allele is the clearest evidence available that the two pools are continuous, and it also shows that the identity values above are lower bounds set by what has been sequenced rather than by what exists.',False)])
RICH([('Two cautions apply to this table. First, values computed against a clustered set understate the closeness of the relationship. In the clustered set MCR-1.1 reached only 62.4%, MCR-2.1 64.1%, MCR-3.1 66.9% and MCR-6.1 65.1%, so clustering had removed the true closest relative of five of the eleven families, in one case by 36 percentage points. Second, partial sequences inflate identity measured over the aligned region. The 400-residue WP_261396260.1 of ',False),('C. freundii',True),(' is 99.0% identical to MCR-9.1 over the part of the protein it covers, which is not comparable with a full-length value and is excluded here.',False)])
RICH([('The families were in most cases more distant from each other than from their chromosomal relatives. Identity between reference alleles ranged from 31.7% to 87.9% with a mean of 41.5% (Figure 2a). Three blocks stood out: MCR-1, MCR-2 and MCR-6 at 81.4% to 87.9%; MCR-3 and MCR-7 at 69.8%; MCR-9 and MCR-10 at 82.9%. Between the blocks, values fell to between 31.7% and 45%.',False)])

H('3.4. The catalytic apparatus was complete before mobilisation',3)
P('MCR-1.1 has a hydrophobic amino-terminal region ending near residue 183 and a periplasmic domain of about 358 residues (Figure 3a, 3b). In the mobilised proteins the predicted membrane-spanning segments numbered three to five and the periplasmic domain occupied between 357 and 382 residues. Across the chromosomal and related proteins the predicted segments ranged from one to five, with 163 of the 176 carrying three or more. Thirteen carry fewer. Only one of those is a short record, the 400-residue partial sequence discussed above; the others are of ordinary length, so the shortfall reflects the limits of a hydropathy heuristic rather than a different architecture.')
RICH([('Of the sixteen positions taken from the MCR-1 structures, six carry the same residue in every sequence in the dataset: Glu246, Thr285, His390, His395, Asp465 and His466 (Table 1). Their invariance is a property of the superfamily and carries no information about mobilisation, and we report it only to establish that no sequence in the dataset is a pseudogene product or a degraded fragment. The other ten vary. Measured against the residue in MCR-1.1 across the 176 chromosomal and related proteins of the expanded dataset, Asn329 differs in 93%, Ser284 in 72%, Cys356 and Cys364 in 49%, Lys333 in 48%, and Cys281, Cys291, Cys414, Cys422 and His478 in 29%. Table 1 reports occupancy in the 150 chromosomal proteins of the primary dataset, a different denominator and therefore a different set of percentages. Two of the ten, Ser284 and Asn329, also vary within the mobilised set, so the remaining eight are treated as the informative set throughout. Eighty-nine of the 176 proteins carry the MCR-1.1 residue at all eight.',False)])
P('Ancestral reconstruction was carried out on those ten positions at the stem node of each mobilised lineage, which is the last node that lineage shares with chromosomal sequences. All six cysteines, Lys333 and His478 were reconstructed with posterior probability 1.00 at all nine stem nodes. The two positions that vary within the mobilised set behaved differently and informatively. Ser284 and Asn329 were reconstructed as alanine and aspartate, the chromosomal configuration, at six of the nine nodes; as serine and asparagine at the node leading to MCR-1, MCR-2 and MCR-6; as alanine and asparagine at the node leading to MCR-12; and as asparagine and glutamine at the node leading to MCR-5, where the posterior probability at position 284 falls to 0.43. The reconstruction is therefore following real variation rather than returning the query sequence. Repeating the analysis on the outgroup-rooted topology of the expanded dataset returned the same residue in all 72 comparisons, eight informative positions across the nine lineages of the primary analysis, so the reconstruction at these positions does not depend on the position of the root.')

